# Security Baseline

Applies to every XingAI repo. Security is not optional and must not be deferred to "after launch".

## Secrets Hygiene

Never commit secrets. No exceptions.

- All credentials go in `.env` (git-ignored) or the host platform's secret manager (Vercel, GitHub Actions secrets).
- `.env.example` contains only safe placeholder values.
- Never use `process.env.SECRET` directly — import from `env.ts` which validates at startup.
- Run `git grep -r "sk-" .` before any public push to catch leaked OpenAI keys.
- Add `.env` to `.gitignore` on project creation. Verify before first push.

## Dependency Scanning

Run on every PR and weekly in CI:

```bash
# Node / Next.js
npm audit --audit-level=high

# Python / FastAPI
pip install pip-audit
pip-audit
```

Failing rules:
- Block merge on `high` or `critical` severity CVEs.
- `moderate` CVEs: create a tracking issue, do not block.
- Update vulnerable packages within 7 days of detection for `high/critical`.

## Input Validation

All data entering the system must be validated before use:

- API request bodies: Zod (`safeParse`) in TypeScript, Pydantic in Python.
- URL params and search params: validate type and length before use.
- User-uploaded files: validate MIME type, size, and extension server-side.
- Never trust `Content-Type` header alone for file uploads.

## SQL / NoSQL Injection

- Always use parameterized queries or ORM methods. Never string-concatenate user input into SQL.
- SQLite (Python): use `?` placeholders, never f-strings in queries.
- Prisma (TS): use Prisma client methods — never `queryRaw` with user input.

## Authentication

- Use NextAuth.js for Next.js apps with Google OAuth as the default provider.
- Never roll custom auth. Never store passwords unless explicitly required.
- JWT secrets must be ≥32 random bytes (`AUTH_SECRET`).
- Session tokens must use `httpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict` cookies.
- Protect all non-public API routes with session checks.
- POCs that skip auth must display a clear "No auth — dev only" banner.

## CORS

FastAPI backends used by XingAI frontends:

```python
from fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware

app.add_middleware(
    CORSMiddleware,
    allow_origins=["https://your-product.xingai.app"],  # never "*" in production
    allow_methods=["GET", "POST"],
    allow_headers=["Content-Type", "Authorization"],
)
```

Never use `allow_origins=["*"]` in production. OK for POC localhost dev only.

## Rate Limiting

Every public API must rate-limit:

- Next.js: use `@upstash/ratelimit` with Redis, or Vercel's built-in rate limiting.
- FastAPI: `slowapi` with IP-based limits.
- Default: 20 requests/minute per IP for AI endpoints (they are expensive).

## Content Security Policy

Add CSP headers to every `*.xingai.app` product:

```ts
// next.config.ts
const cspHeader = `
  default-src 'self';
  script-src 'self' 'unsafe-eval' 'unsafe-inline';
  style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';
  img-src 'self' blob: data: https:;
  font-src 'self';
  connect-src 'self' https://api.openai.com;
  frame-ancestors 'none';
`.replace(/\n/g, ' ');
```

## Pre-Push Security Checklist

```markdown
- [ ] No secrets in staged files (`git diff --staged | grep -i "sk-\|secret\|password\|token"`)
- [ ] npm audit / pip-audit passes at high severity
- [ ] Input validated with Zod or Pydantic at all entry points
- [ ] No raw process.env reads — using env.ts
- [ ] Auth enforced on all non-public routes
- [ ] CORS restricted to known origins in production
- [ ] Rate limiting on AI/expensive endpoints
```

## Common Mistakes

- `.env` committed instead of `.env.example`.
- `allow_origins=["*"]` shipped to production.
- JWT secret is a short, guessable string.
- File upload validates only client-side MIME type.
- SQL query built with f-string + user input.
- AI endpoint has no rate limit — one user can exhaust OpenAI budget.
